{"kind":"propose","text":"Evaluate merge staleness as of the approval, not as of now. A design for the staleness footgun, offered for ratification, with the two obvious fixes rejected on evidence and the reason each is wrong.\n\nWHY THIS IS URGENT RATHER THAN TIDY. On 2026-08-12 a basis that looked prescribed made a head unmergeable four times in one afternoon, each with a different plausible anchor: the predecessor at the same path, the motivating request, the successor being repaired, and the assert recording the defect. The sharpest case cost a reviewed head: codex approved fd3fb7b5 at 15:46; a step-5 retirement they had themselves ruled correct then staled 02b730f9, which staled the candidate 30284a32, which staled the approval, and gs merge refused it at 15:52 over code that had not changed by a byte. Ordering could not have avoided it, because the two heads in flight touched one path from opposite ends.\n\nThe repair for a red main hit the same wall: it rested on the successor whose predecessor's retirement it was repairing, so it inherited staleness from the thing it was fixing.\n\nFIRST FIX CONSIDERED, AND WHY NOT. Make KindArtifact terminal for staleness, so a retired artifact still stales its dependents but a merely-stale one does not pass it on. The machinery already exists — kinds carry propagates, terminal and exempt. Modelled against the log at depth 4591: 367 records stop being stale, all 39 live artifacts resting directly on a retired record stay stale, and the four incidents above clear while a30c9d84 and 8aa25919 correctly stay stale because their direct basis really was withdrawn.\n\nIt fails a test that is right. TestRetiredArtifactMarksDependentsAsDescribingASupersededWorld pins that a retired artifact under a document marks dependents across intermediate hops, and its comment says why: only that kind of staleness means go and re-read the code. Multi-hop propagation is the documentation signal working. Cutting it to fix the merge gate would trade a real feature for an unrelated one.\n\nSECOND FIX CONSIDERED, AND WHY NOT. Let gs merge report staleness instead of refusing it, keeping the refusal for retirement. The seam is already there: standingArtifact and standingStatement refuse what was retired and report staleness, and liveArtifact and liveStatement add the refusal, used only by validateMerge.\n\nIt fails a test that is also right. TestMergeGuardStillRefusesAMovedWorldAfterAnApprovedReview says merge keeps the strict reading that review has given up, because this is the step that moves main with nobody weighing anything. That is a considered position and I am not going to overturn it by deleting its test.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES BOTH OBJECTIONS. Staleness at merge should be evaluated as of the approval's own position in the log, not as of the current frontier.\n\nA verdict is a claim about an exact immutable head made at a moment. If a basis was retired before that moment, the reviewer signed over a world that had already moved, and refusing is exactly right — that is what the strict reading protects. If it was retired after, nothing the reviewer weighed has changed: the commit is immutable, the artifact still names it, the diff they read is the diff that would land. Refusing there is not strictness, it is a race against unrelated bookkeeping, and it is the only case any of today's four incidents fall into.\n\nThis keeps the documentation signal untouched, because the fold's propagation does not change. It keeps merge strict against a moved world, because a pre-existing retirement still refuses. It removes the footgun entirely, because succession after the verdict cannot reach back.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS, honestly. The fold must expose when a retirement took effect, not only that it did, so staleness can be asked as of a position. That is more than the one-word change the first option would have been, and it needs a fold profile bump because the projection gains a field. It also needs a decision about acts filed between the verdict and the merge that are neither: a retirement of the artifact itself after approval should still refuse, because that is a withdrawn pointer rather than a moved world, and withdrawal is not time-bound.\n\nThis answers ac8ef0de, which asked for exactly this shape — merge validity temporal and review-frontier-bound — and it is the same request be558440 makes from the other end. I have not implemented it. Hugh asked to prioritise the staleness footguns, and I would rather have the shape ratified than cut a third design against two deliberate positions I have already been wrong against once today."}